111","file":"preprints/A-positive-solution-to-Tingleys-problem-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","line":51,"producer_hash_method":"atlas-whitespace-normalized-text","producer_statement_sha256":"b131090919cab4a60a1e6256ffc6c323a6f5b595dc7cd379cfd386f8e049d852","provenance":{"kind":"computed","pin":"fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb","source":"preprints/A-positive-solution-to-Tingleys-problem-September-23-2026/build/main.tex"},"sha256":"sha256:6e0588ce2f69dd01a0dee0196575a381d3396830343fb03340fcadf244073111","url":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/A-positive-solution-to-Tingleys-problem-September-23-2026/build/main.tex#L51"},"statement_kind":"theorem","text":"\\label{thm:main}\nLet $X,Y$ be nonzero real Banach spaces and let $f:S_X\\to S_Y$ be a surjective isometry. Then\n\\[\nT(0)=0,\\qquad T(x)=\\norm{x}f\\left(\\frac{x}{\\norm{x}}\\right)\\quad(x\\ne0)\n\\]\nis a surjective real-linear isometry. It is the unique linear extension of $f$."}
